'''The Movement''' was a term coined in 1954 by J. D. Scott, [[literary editor]] of ''[[The Spectator]]'', to describe a group of writers including [[Philip Larkin]], [[Kingsley Amis]], [[Donald Davie]], [[D. J. Enright]], [[John Wain]], [[Elizabeth Jennings (poet)|Elizabeth Jennings]], [[Thom Gunn]] and [[Robert Conquest]]. The Movement was quintessentially English in character; poets from other parts of the United Kingdom were not involved.

