'''Oasis''' are<!--Do not change anything to past tense unless the group has confirmed to have disbanded again post-tour. Alec McKinlay's claims have been disputed by Liam. Use neutral present-tense language until their future is confirmed.--> an English [[Rock music|rock]] band formed in [[Manchester]] in 1991. The group initially consisted of [[Liam Gallagher]] (lead vocals), [[Paul "Bonehead" Arthurs]] (guitar), [[Paul "Guigsy" McGuigan]] (bass guitar) and [[Tony McCarroll]] (drums). Liam asked his older brother [[Noel Gallagher]] (lead guitar, vocals) to join as a fifth member a few months later to finalise their formation. Noel became the ''[[de facto]]'' leader of the group and took over the songwriting duties for the band's first four albums.<ref>{{Cite web |url=https://www.nytimes.com/2024/08/27/arts/music/oasis-reunion-gallagher-breakup-feud.html |title=Oasis: Timeline of a Sibling Rivalry for the Ages|date=27 August 2024|first=Victor|last=Mather|work=[[The New York Times]]|access-date=28 June 2025}}</ref><ref>{{Cite web |url=https://variety.com/2024/music/news/oasis-break-up-liam-noel-gallagher-fights-1236118668/ |title=Oasis at War: Liam and Noel Gallagher's 10 Biggest Fights|date=27 August 2024|first=A.D.|last=Amorosi|work=Variety|access-date=28 June 2025}}</ref> They are regarded as the most globally successful group of the [[Britpop]] era and one of the most influential rock bands of all time.<ref>{{cite web|url=https://www.rockhall.com/oasis|archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20240302042410/https://www.rockhall.com/oasis|url-status=dead|archive-date=2024-03-02|title=Oasis Bio|website=Rock & Roll Hall of Fame}}</ref>

Oasis signed to independent record label [[Creation Records]] in 1993 and released their record-setting debut album ''[[Definitely Maybe]]'' (1994), which topped the [[UK Albums Chart]] and quickly became the fastest-selling debut album in British history at the time. The following year, they released follow up album ''[[(What's the Story) Morning Glory?]]'' (1995) with new drummer [[Alan White (Oasis drummer)|Alan "Whitey" White]] in the midst of a highly publicised chart rivalry with peers [[Blur (band)|Blur]], dubbed by the British media as the "[[Battle of Britpop]]". Spending ten weeks at number one on the British charts, ''(What's the Story) Morning Glory?'' was also an international chart success and became one of the [[best-selling albums of all time]], the [[List of best-selling albums in the United Kingdom|UK's third-best-selling album]], and the [[List of best-selling albums of the 1990s in the United Kingdom|UK's best-selling album of the 1990s]]. The Gallagher brothers featured regularly in [[tabloid newspapers]] throughout the 1990s for their public disputes and wild lifestyles. In 1996, Oasis performed two nights at [[Concerts at Knebworth House|Knebworth]] for an audience of 125,000 each time, the largest outdoor concerts in UK history at the time. In 1997, Oasis released their highly anticipated third album, ''[[Be Here Now (album)|Be Here Now]]'', which became the fastest-selling album in UK chart history selling 696,000 copies in its first week.

Founding members Arthurs and McGuigan left in 1999 during the recording of the band's fourth album, ''[[Standing on the Shoulder of Giants]]'' (2000). They were replaced by former [[Heavy Stereo]] guitarist [[Gem Archer]] on guitar and [[Ride (band)|Ride]] guitarist [[Andy Bell (Welsh musician)|Andy Bell]] on bass guitar. White was asked to leave the band in 2003<ref>{{cite news |last1=Leonard |first1=Michael |title=Steve White slams Oasis treatment of drummer |url=https://www.musicradar.com/news/guitars/steve-white-slams-oasis-treatment-of-drummer-165921 |access-date=14 July 2026 |work=MusicRadar |date=21 July 2008 |language=en}}</ref><ref>{{cite news |title=ALAN WHITE'S DEPARTURE FROM OASIS CONFIRMED |url=https://www.nme.com/news/music/oasis-841-1371613 |access-date=14 July 2026 |work=NME |date=16 January 2004}}</ref> and replaced by touring member [[Zak Starkey]] the following year. Oasis released three more albums in the 2000s: ''[[Heathen Chemistry]]'' (2002), ''[[Don't Believe the Truth]]'' (2005) and ''[[Dig Out Your Soul]]'' (2008) which all reached number one in the UK album charts with the last two releases of their original run being regarded as a return to form for the band. The group abruptly disbanded in 2009 after the sudden departure of Noel Gallagher. The remaining members of the band continued under the name [[Beady Eye]] until their disbandment in 2014. Both Gallagher brothers have since had successful solo careers. Oasis reformed in 2024 and concurrently announced the [[Oasis Live '25 Tour]], which they embarked on the following year.<ref>{{Cite web |last=Trendell |first=Andrew |date=2024-08-27 |title="The great wait is over" – Oasis announce 2025 UK and Ireland reunion tour |url=https://www.nme.com/news/music/oasis-reunion-tour-uk-ireland-stadium-2025-liam-noel-gallagher-tickets-3787539 |access-date=2025-07-12 |website=NME |language=en-GB}}</ref> The band consists of Liam and Noel Gallagher, Bonehead, Archer and Bell.

As of 2026, Oasis have sold over 100 million records worldwide,<ref>{{cite web|url=https://rockhall.com/oasis/|archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20260227230942/https://rockhall.com/oasis/|url-status=dead|archive-date=2026-02-27|title=Oasis Bio|website=Rock & Roll Hall of Fame}}</ref> making them one of the [[best-selling music artists]] of all time.<ref>{{cite web|url=https://www.bbc.com/culture/article/20240827-why-oasis-defined-the-spirit-of-90s-britain|title=Definitely Maybe at 30: Why Oasis defined the spirit of 90s Britain|website=BBC|date=27 August 2024}}</ref><ref>{{cite web|url=https://www.nbcnews.com/news/world/oasis-announce-reunion-tour-15-years-warring-gallagher-broth-rcna168186|title=Oasis announce major reunion tour 15 years after warring Gallagher brothers split|website=NBC News|date=27 August 2024}}</ref><ref>{{cite web|url=https://www.forbes.com/sites/conormurray/2024/08/28/oasis-surges-on-spotify-and-apple-music-as-gallaghers-plan-surprise-reunion-tour/|title=Oasis Surges On Spotify And Apple Music—As Gallaghers Plan Surprise Reunion Tour|website=Forbes|date=28 August 2024}}</ref> They are among the most successful acts in the history of the [[UK singles chart]] and the [[UK Albums Chart]], with eight UK number-one singles and eight UK number-one albums.<ref name="GWR2010">{{cite web|url=http://community.guinnessworldrecords.com/_Oasis-Coldplay-Take-That-enter-Guinness-World-Records-2010-Book/blog/713512/7691.html|title=Oasis, Coldplay & Take That enter Guinness World Records 2010 Book – Guinness World Records Blog post|publisher=Community.guinnessworldrecords.com|url-status=dead|archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20100124221745/http://community.guinnessworldrecords.com/_Oasis-Coldplay-Take-That-enter-Guinness-World-Records-2010-Book/blog/713512/7691.html|archive-date=24 January 2010|access-date=23 June 2010}}</ref><ref>{{cite web|url=https://www.nme.com/news/oasis/24788|title=Oasis receive Outstanding Brit Award|date=19 October 2006|work=NME|access-date=28 September 2014|archive-date=22 October 2014|archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20141022202938/http://www.nme.com/news/oasis/24788|url-status=live}}</ref><ref>{{Cite web|url=https://www.oasisinet.com/|archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20150825180037/http://www.oasisinet.com/|url-status=dead|title=The Official Oasis Website &#124; Oasis Be Here Now reissue|archive-date=25 August 2015|website=Oasis}}</ref> The band also achieved three [[Recording Industry Association of America]] (RIAA)-certified [[RIAA certification|Platinum]] albums in the US. They won 17 [[NME Awards|''NME'' Awards]], nine [[Q Awards]], four [[MTV Europe Music Awards]], one [[MTV Video Music Awards Japan|MTV Japan Music Award]], two [[Ivor Novello Awards]] for songwriting, three [[World Music Awards]], two [[UK Music Video Awards]] and seven [[Brit Awards]], including one in 2007 for Outstanding Contribution to Music, one in 2010 for the Best Album of the Last 30 Years for ''(What's the Story) Morning Glory?'' and in 2026, Noel Gallagher received Songwriter of the Year. They were also nominated for three [[Grammy Award]]s.<ref>[https://www.grammy.com/artists/oasis/8322 "GRAMMY Award Results for Oasis"] . National Academy of Recording Arts and Sciences. Retrieved 9 September 2019</ref> They were inducted into the [[Rock and Roll Hall of Fame]] in 2026.<ref name="BBCRnR">{{Cite news |title=Oasis among record number of Brits entering Rock & Roll Hall of Fame |url=https://www.bbc.com/news/articles/c9qd5537zxpo |publisher=BBC.com}}</ref>

